  <data name="UIModel_Cursors_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Download and install the "Windows 11 Cursors Concept v2" cursors from Jepri Creations. An Internet connection is required. [View the cursors on DeviantArt](https://www.deviantart.com/jepricreations/art/Windows-11-Cursors-Concept-886489356)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_FolderGroupBy_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Group files and folders in the Downloads folder</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_FolderGroupBy_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Do not group</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_FolderGroupBy_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>By date modified</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_NavigationPaneExpand_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatically expand to current folder in the navigation pane of File Explorer</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RecentlyAddedStartApps_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show recently added apps on Start</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_MostUsedStartApps_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show most used apps on Start</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartRecommendedSection_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show the Recommended section on Start</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartRecommendationsTips_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show recommendations for tips, shortcuts, new apps, and more on Start</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartAccountNotifications_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show Microsoft account-related notifications on Start</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartLayout_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Start layout</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartLayout_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Default</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartLayout_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>More pins</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StartLayout_Title_3" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>More recommendations</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_OneDrive_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>OneDrive</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_OneDrive_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The OneDrive user folder won't be removed if any files are found there. An Internet connection is required to download the OneDrive installer from the Microsoft server.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StorageSense_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Storage Sense</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StorageSense_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatically free up space and delete temporary files using the built-in Windows cleanup when disk space is low.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_Hibernation_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable hibernation</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_Hibernation_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Hibernation is a power-saving state used primarily on laptops. Unlike sleep, hibernation saves your open documents and programs to the `%SystemDrive%\hiberfil.sys` file on the system drive and turns off your computer. By default, the size of the hidden protected system file `%SystemDrive%\hiberfil.sys` is 40% of the total amount of RAM installed. Disabling hibernation will also disable fast startup, and Hibernate will no longer be shown in the Power menu. It is not recommended to turn hibernation off on laptops.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_Win32LongPathsSupport_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Windows long path support</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_Win32LongPathsSupport_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>By default, a path in Windows is limited to 260 characters. With long path support, programs that are built for it can create and open files with longer paths. Programs that do not support long paths still have the limit.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_BSoDStopError_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Display the Stop error parameters when a BSoD occurs</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_BSoDStopError_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>By default, Windows shows only the name of the Stop error when a BSoD occurs. With this setting, the blue screen also shows the parameters of the error, which help to identify the cause of a BSoD.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_AdminApprovalMode_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Configure UAC behavior</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_AdminApprovalMode_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Notify me only when apps try to make changes to my computer</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_AdminApprovalMode_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Never notify</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_AdminApprovalMode_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When User Account Control (UAC) is enabled, Windows prompts for consent or prompts for credentials of a valid local administrator account before starting a program or task that requires a full administrator access token. This prompt ensures that no malicious software can be silently installed or run. With "Never notify", apps can make changes to your computer without asking you, so this protection is turned off. Not recommended.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DeliveryOptimization_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Windows Update Delivery Optimization</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DeliveryOptimization_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delivery Optimization lets your PC get Windows updates and Microsoft Store apps from other PCs on your local network or on the Internet that have already downloaded them, and send them to other PCs in the same way. It reduces the Internet traffic when several devices are updated and makes downloads more reliable on a slow connection.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WindowsManageDefaultPrinter_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Let Windows manage my default printer</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WindowsManageDefaultPrinter_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>By default, Windows sets the last used printer as the default one.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_UpdateMicrosoftProducts_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Receive updates for other Microsoft products</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_UpdateMicrosoftProducts_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Get updates for other Microsoft products, such as Microsoft 365 (formerly Office), together with Windows updates.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RestartNotification_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Notify me when a restart is required to finish updating</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RestartNotification_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show a notification when your device requires a restart to finish updating.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RestartDeviceAfterUpdate_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restart as soon as possible to finish updating</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RestartDeviceAfterUpdate_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restart as soon as possible (even during active hours) to finish updating, and notify 15 minutes before restarting.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ActiveHours_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Adjust active hours for Windows Update</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ActiveHours_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatically</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ActiveHours_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Manually</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ActiveHours_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>You can set active hours to let Windows know when you usually use your PC to prevent automatic restarts after a Windows update. The default active hours are from 8:00 AM to 5:00 PM.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WindowsLatestUpdate_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Get the latest Windows updates as soon as they're available</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WindowsLatestUpdate_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Get the latest non-security Windows updates, fixes, and improvements as they roll out. If any are available, Windows will start downloading them immediately after you apply this setting.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_PowerPlan_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose a power plan</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_PowerPlan_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>High performance</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_PowerPlan_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Balanced</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_PowerPlan_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The "High performance" power plan is not recommended for laptops. Also, enabling it doesn't affect your FPS: it only manages how your PC uses power.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_NetworkAdaptersSavePower_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Allow the computer to turn off the active network adapters to save power</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_NetworkAdaptersSavePower_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Only the currently used network adapter will be configured, even if your motherboard has an additional Wi-Fi adapter alongside an Ethernet one. **The network adapter will be restarted, so the connection will be lost for a few seconds.** It is not recommended to turn this off on laptops.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InputMethod_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose the default input method (keyboard layout)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InputMethod_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>English (United States)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InputMethod_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use language list</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InputMethod_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>By default, Windows starts with the first input method of your language list. Choose English to start with the English (United States) keyboard layout instead, whatever language is first in the list.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WinPrtScrFolder_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose the default folder for screenshots taken with Win+PrtScn or Win+Shift+S</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WinPrtScrFolder_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Desktop</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WinPrtScrFolder_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pictures</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RecommendedTroubleshooting_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Recommended troubleshooting</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RecommendedTroubleshooting_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Run troubleshooters automatically, then notify</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RecommendedTroubleshooting_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Ask me before running troubleshooters</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RecommendedTroubleshooting_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Microsoft can recommend and run troubleshooters to fix issues. How much do you want Microsoft to help when there are issues on your device that troubleshooters might be able to fix? For this feature to work, the diagnostic data level will be set to "Optional diagnostic data", and Windows Error Reporting will be turned on.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ReservedStorage_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Reserved Storage</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ReservedStorage_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows reserves some storage (~7 GB) to ensure proper performance and successful updates of your device. After disabling, the reserved storage will be released after the next update is installed.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_F1HelpPage_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable help lookup via F1</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_F1HelpPage_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>By default, pressing F1 in Windows opens a help page in the default web browser, which may result in opening the browser accidentally.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_NumLock_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Num Lock at startup</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_CapsLock_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Caps Lock key</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_CapsLock_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A restart is required.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StickyShift_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Press the Shift key 5 times to turn on Sticky keys</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_StickyShift_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pressing the Shift key 5 times in a row brings up a pop-up that may interrupt your work.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_Autoplay_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Use AutoPlay for all media and devices</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_Autoplay_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When you insert a removable device such as a USB flash drive or a memory card into your PC, Windows shows the "AutoPlay" pop-up.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ThumbnailCacheRemoval_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable thumbnail cache removal</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_ThumbnailCacheRemoval_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows automatically deletes the thumbnail cache after a restart or shutdown via the "SilentCleanup" scheduled task, and File Explorer has to re-create thumbnails. You can disable thumbnail cache removal to keep the cache from being deleted automatically. The thumbnail cache is stored as .db files in the `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\Windows\Explorer` folder.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_SaveRestartableApps_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Automatically save my restartable apps and restart them when I sign back in</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_SaveRestartableApps_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>If a program was run as administrator in the previous session, it will be restarted without admin rights.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RestorePreviousFolders_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restore previous folder windows at logon</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RestorePreviousFolders_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Restore all the extra tabs which you previously had open in each File Explorer window.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DefaultTerminalApp_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Default terminal app</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DefaultTerminalApp_Title_1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows Terminal</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DefaultTerminalApp_Title_2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Console Host</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DefaultTerminalApp_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The terminal application that launches when a command-line application is run without an existing session, for example from the Start menu or the Run dialog.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InstallVisualC_x64_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable Packages 2017–2026 x64</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InstallVisualC_x64_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The installer will be downloaded from the Microsoft server (an Internet connection is required).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InstallVisualC_x86_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable Packages 2017–2026 x86</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InstallVisualC_x86_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The installer will be downloaded from the Microsoft server (an Internet connection is required).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InstallDotNetRuntime_11_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>.NET Desktop Runtime 11 x64</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_InstallDotNetRuntime_11_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>.NET is the successor to .NET Framework. You need to install .NET 11 if an app was built for this version, even if other .NET versions are already installed. .NET 11 is an STS (Standard Term Support) release. The installer will be downloaded from the Microsoft server (an Internet connection is required).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RemoveWindowsAI_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Remove Microsoft Copilot and Recall</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RemoveWindowsAI_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Uninstall the Microsoft Copilot app and turn off the Recall component of Windows. If you clear the check box, Recall is turned on again and the Microsoft Copilot page is opened in Microsoft Store.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RegistryBackup_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable automatic backup of the Windows registry</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_RegistryBackup_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Starting with Windows 10 version 1803, Windows no longer automatically backs up the Windows registry to the `%SystemRoot%\System32\config\RegBack` folder. Enabling registry backups may help you restore Windows in extreme cases.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UwpPage_AboutAppIcon.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose the UWP apps to uninstall. The list is not prepared in advance: it is built in real time with the apps' native icons and localized names taken from Windows.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UwpPage_UwpAppsNotFound.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No UWP apps to uninstall.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UwpPage_ForAllUsers.Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show packages for all users</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_XBOXGameBar_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable XBOX Game Bar</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_XBOXGameBar_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Turn it off to prevent the "You'll need a new app to open this ms-gamingoverlay" pop-up message, even if you uninstalled the XBOX Game Bar app before.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_XBOXGameTips_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show XBOX Game Bar tips</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_GPUScheduling_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable hardware-accelerated GPU scheduling</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_GPUScheduling_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>With hardware-accelerated GPU scheduling, the graphics card manages its own video memory and the order of the work it gets, which takes some load off the CPU and may reduce latency. Do not expect a significant impact on performance. It works only with a dedicated GPU and WDDM version 2.7 or higher. A restart is required.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskSchedulerPage_AboutTasks.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The scheduled tasks below are sufficient to keep Windows clean. There is no need to constantly clean other system folders, as is often advised on the Internet. The tasks respect the **Do not disturb** mode: their notifications won't pop up while you are playing a game, watching a video in full screen, or when the mode is turned on.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Page_AboutPolicies.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>SophiApp for Windows keeps the use of policies to a minimum. The settings that do create policies also show them in the Local Group Policy Editor snap-in (`gpedit.msc`), if it is available in your Windows edition.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_CleanupTask_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create Windows Cleanup scheduled task</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_CleanupTask_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create the "Windows Cleanup" task and the "Windows Cleanup Notification" task, which shows a pop-up notification, in the Sophia folder of Task Scheduler to clean up unused files and Windows updates. Every 30 days a native notification prompts you to run the cleanup, and another one appears when it completes.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_WindowsCleanup_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleaning up unused Windows files and updates using the built-in Disk Cleanup app. The scheduled task can run only if the user "{0}" is signed in.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_WindowsCleanupNotification_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A pop-up notification reminder about cleaning up unused Windows files and updates. The scheduled task can run only if the user "{0}" is signed in.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_WindowsCleanupToast_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Run the task to clean up unused Windows files and updates?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_WindowsCleanupToast_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows cleanup</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_SoftwareDistributionTask_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create SoftwareDistribution scheduled task</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_SoftwareDistributionTask_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create the "SoftwareDistribution" task in the Sophia folder of Task Scheduler to clean up the `%SystemRoot%\SoftwareDistribution\Download` folder, where Windows update installation files are downloaded. The task waits until the Windows Update service has finished running. It runs every 90 days and shows a native Windows notification when it completes.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_SoftwareDistribution_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleaning up the Windows update cache folder "{1}". The scheduled task can run only if the user "{0}" is signed in.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_SoftwareDistributionToast_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The Windows update cache was successfully deleted.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_TempTask_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create Temp scheduled task</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_TempTask_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create the "Temp" task in the Sophia folder of Task Scheduler to clean up the `%TEMP%` folder. Only files older than one day are deleted. The task runs every 60 days and shows a native Windows notification when it completes.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_TempTaskToast_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The temporary folder was successfully cleaned up.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskScheduler_TempTask_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Cleaning up the temporary folder "{1}" (%TEMP%). The scheduled task can run only if the user "{0}" is signed in.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SecurityPage_OpenEventViewer.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Open Event Viewer</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Panel_RestartReminder.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Make sure to restart your PC.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TaskSchedulerPage_OpenTaskScheduler.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Open Task Scheduler</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_NetworkProtection_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Microsoft Defender Exploit Guard network protection</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_NetworkProtection_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Network protection helps prevent users from using any application to access dangerous domains that may host phishing scams, exploits, and other malicious content on the Internet.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_PUAppsDetection_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable detection of potentially unwanted applications and block them with Microsoft Defender</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_PUAppsDetection_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Potentially unwanted applications are not malware, but they can show unexpected ads, install other software, or slow your PC down.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DefenderSandbox_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable sandboxing for Microsoft Defender</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_DefenderSandbox_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Running Microsoft Defender in a sandbox ensures that in the unlikely event of a compromise, malicious actions are limited to the isolated environment, protecting the rest of the system from harm. A restart is required.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_EventViewerCustomView_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create the "Process Creation" custom view in Event Viewer</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_EventViewerCustomView_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable logging of running processes, including their arguments, and PowerShell scripts, and create the custom "Process Creation" view in Event Viewer. Event Viewer will be closed if it is open.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_EventViewerCustomView_QueryDescription" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Track launched processes and PowerShell activity</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_EventViewerCustomView_QueryName" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Process Creation</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_AppsSmartScreen_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Check apps and files with Microsoft Defender SmartScreen</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_SaveZoneInformation_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mark files that have been downloaded from the Internet as unsafe</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_SaveZoneInformation_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Attachment Manager is included in Windows to protect your PC from unsafe files downloaded from the Internet. If Attachment Manager identifies an attachment that might be unsafe, it prevents (blocks) you from opening the file or warns you before you open it. The metadata is saved as an Alternate Data Stream (ADS). If you wish to unblock a downloaded file manually, right-click it, select Properties, and click "Unblock".</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WindowsSandbox_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable Windows Sandbox</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_WindowsSandbox_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows Sandbox provides a lightweight desktop environment to safely run applications in isolation. Software installed inside the Windows Sandbox environment remains sandboxed and runs separately from the host machine. A sandbox is temporary: when it's closed, all software, files, and state are deleted. You get a brand-new instance of the sandbox every time you open the application. Available only in the Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions. Virtualization must be turned on in the UEFI settings. A restart is required.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_LocalSecurityAuthority_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enable protection for the Local Security Authority (LSA) process without UEFI lock to prevent code injection</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_LocalSecurityAuthority_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Protection for the Local Security Authority (LSA) prevents non-protected processes from reading its memory and injecting code. This feature provides added security for the credentials that LSA stores and manages. A restart is required.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="UIModel_MSIExtractContext_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show the "Extract all" item in the Windows Installer (.msi) context menu</value>
